  "reconsidered_answer": "Your plan is a highly pragmatic and structured way forward. Listening to the revised cut before deciding on postponement ensures your choice is grounded in the actual material rather than an abstract debate. Documenting the precise interpretive differences, if any remain, keeps your feedback focused and protects the discussion from devolving into an argument about creative control.

On credit, your proposal of equal top-line billing with detailed role descriptions below is a strong compromise. However, your hesitation is entirely valid: if Jonah continues to treat major structural choices as his exclusive domain, a shared top-line credit may feel like an empty gesture that masks a deeper imbalance in authority.

It is natural to find it difficult to separate this project from the prospects of a future one. While you do not need to establish rules for a next collaboration before the showcase, you do need to accept that how this project ends will inevitably shape your willingness to work together again. You can agree to treat this project's resolution as a time-bound compromise to meet your immediate six-week deadline, but any future partnership will require explicit boundaries regarding authority set from day one.",
